We want to divide $x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9$ by $x^2-3x+2$.
Since the divisor is a quadratic, we can perform polynomial long division.
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^2} & & +2 \\
\cline{2-6}
x^2-3x+2 & x^4 & -3x^3 & +4x^2 & +11x & -9 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^4} & -3x^3 & +2x^2 \\
\cline{2-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & 0 & 2x^2 & +11x & -9 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 2x^2 & -6x & +4 \\
\cline{4-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 0 & 17x & -13 \\
\end{array}
\]
The quotient is $x^2+2$ and the remainder is $17x-13$.

Alternatively, we can use synthetic division to divide by $x^2-3x+2=0$, which gives $x^2 = 3x-2$.
$x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9 = x^2(x^2) - 3x^3+4x^2+11x-9 = x^2(3x-2) - 3x^3+4x^2+11x-9$
$= 3x^3 - 2x^2 - 3x^3+4x^2+11x-9 = 2x^2+11x-9 = 2(3x-2)+11x-9 = 6x-4+11x-9 = 17x-13$.
Since the divisor is quadratic and the remainder is linear, this means that $x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9 = (x^2-3x+2)(x^2+2) + 17x-13$.
Thus the remainder is $17x-13$.

\boxed{17x-13}

